      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1862 Battle of Glorieta Pass, fought March 26–28, halted a Confederate Texas invasion, earning Glorieta a place in Civil War lore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/glorieta_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Parts of the Civil War battlefield survive inside Pecos National Historical Park, though modern I‑25 development has reshaped some original terrain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/glorieta_new_mexico",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Archaeologists find Paleo‑Indian stone tools near Glorieta, indicating hunters chased bison there as far back as 7,500 BCE.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/glorieta_new_mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Around 1300 CE, Towa‑speaking Ancestral Puebloans built Pecos Pueblo at Glorieta Pass’s eastern entrance, linking Rio Grande Valley traders with Great Plains groups.",
